replies.text:
I'll analyze EVAL-02 for security threats using STRIDE methodology.

Based on the context provided, this is a partner file drop system where partners upload CSV files of orders (potentially several gigabytes) to an SFTP server, which are then parsed and imported into an orders database.

Here's the threat model:

```json
{
  "schema": "redline.threatmodel.v1",
  "issueKey": "EVAL-02",
  "sketch": {
    "components": ["SFTP Server", "Parser Job", "Orders Database", "Partner Upload Client", "File Storage"],
    "dataStores": ["Orders Database", "SFTP File Storage"],
    "externalServices": ["Partner Systems"],
    "trustBoundaries": ["Partner Network to SFTP", "SFTP to Parser", "Parser to Database"],
    "dataClasses": [],
    "exposure": "partner",
    "llmFeatures": false
  },
  "threats": [
    {
      "stride": "Tampering",
      "title": "SQL injection through malicious CSV content",
      "scenario": "An attacker uploads a CSV file containing SQL injection payloads in order fields. When the parser imports the file, these payloads execute against the database, potentially allowing the attacker to read, modify, or delete arbitrary data, or take over the database server.",
      "component": "Parser Job",
      "likelihood": 4,
      "impact": 5,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "quote": "A parser job imports each file into the orders database."
        }
      ],
      "asvs": ["V5.3.4"],
      "cwe": ["CWE-89"],
      "plainLanguage": "An attacker could sneak database commands into the CSV that run when the file is processed, potentially taking over the database.",
      "requirement": {
        "title": "Use parameterized queries for all database operations",
        "given": "the parser processes CSV files",
        "when": "inserting order data into the database",
        "then": "it must use parameterized queries or an ORM that prevents SQL injection, never string concatenation"
      }
    },
    {
      "stride": "Tampering",
      "title": "CSV formula injection in order data",
      "scenario": "An attacker uploads a CSV with formula injection payloads (e.g., =cmd|'/c calc', @SUM(1+1)) in order fields. When staff open the exported order data in Excel or similar tools, these formulas execute arbitrary commands on their machine, potentially installing malware or exfiltrating sensitive data.",
      "component": "Parser Job",
      "likelihood": 4,
      "impact": 4,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "quote": "Partners upload CSV files of orders to our SFTP server."
        }
      ],
      "asvs": ["V5.2.2"],
      "cwe": ["CWE-1236"],
      "plainLanguage": "An attacker could hide executable commands in the CSV that run when someone opens it in Excel, potentially taking over their computer.",
      "requirement": {
        "title": "Sanitize all CSV output by escaping formula characters",
        "given": "order data may be viewed in spreadsheet applications",
        "when": "storing or exporting CSV data",
        "then": "prefix cells starting with =, +, -, @, tab, or carriage return with a single quote to prevent formula execution"
      }
    },
    {
      "stride": "DenialOfService",
      "title": "Resource exhaustion through large file uploads",
      "scenario": "An attacker with partner credentials uploads extremely large CSV files (at or near the gigabyte limit) or multiple large files simultaneously. This exhausts disk space on the SFTP server or overwhelms the parser, causing service disruption and preventing legitimate partners from uploading files or delaying order processing.",
      "component": "SFTP Server",
      "likelihood": 4,
      "impact": 4,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "quote": "Files can be up to several gigabytes."
        }
      ],
      "asvs": ["V1.5.2", "V12.1.1"],
      "cwe": ["CWE-400"],
      "plainLanguage": "Someone could upload huge files to fill up the server's disk space or slow down processing, preventing legitimate orders from being imported.",
      "requirement": {
        "title": "Enforce per-partner upload quotas and rate limits",
        "given": "files can be very large",
        "when": "partners upload files",
        "then": "enforce a maximum file size (e.g., 2GB), maximum files per hour (e.g., 10), and maximum total storage per partner (e.g., 50GB), rejecting uploads that exceed these limits"
      }
    },
    {
      "stride": "InformationDisclosure",
      "title": "Cross-partner file access through weak directory isolation",
      "scenario": "The SFTP server stores all partners' files in a shared directory structure without proper access controls. An attacker who compromises one partner's credentials can navigate the directory tree and download other partners' CSV files, exposing their order data including customer information and business patterns.",
      "component": "SFTP Server",
      "likelihood": 3,
      "impact": 5,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "quote": "Partners upload CSV files of orders to our SFTP server."
        }
      ],
      "asvs": ["V4.1.1"],
      "cwe": ["CWE-639"],
      "plainLanguage": "If one partner account is hacked, the attacker might be able to see other partners' uploaded files and steal their order data.",
      "requirement": {
        "title": "Isolate each partner's files with chroot jails",
        "given": "multiple partners use the same SFTP server",
        "when": "a partner connects",
        "then": "use SFTP chroot to restrict them to their own directory, preventing directory traversal to other partners' files or system directories"
      }
    },
    {
      "stride": "ElevationOfPrivilege",
      "title": "Command injection through malicious filenames",
      "scenario": "An attacker uploads a file with a malicious filename containing shell metacharacters (e.g., 'orders; rm -rf /.csv' or '$(wget malware).csv'). If the parser or cleanup scripts process filenames unsafely (e.g., in shell commands), this executes arbitrary commands on the server, potentially giving the attacker full control.",
      "component": "Parser Job",
      "likelihood": 3,
      "impact": 5,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "quote": "A parser job imports each file into the orders database."
        }
      ],
      "asvs": ["V12.3.1"],
      "cwe": ["CWE-78"],
      "plainLanguage": "An attacker could name the file with commands that run on the server when the parser processes it, potentially taking over the system.",
      "requirement": {
        "title": "Validate and sanitize filenames before processing",
        "given": "untrusted partners control filenames",
        "when": "the parser accesses uploaded files",
        "then": "validate filenames against a strict allowlist (alphanumeric, dash, underscore, single dot), reject others, and never pass filenames to shell commands or use them in path operations without validation"
      }
    },
    {
      "stride": "Spoofing",
      "title": "Partner account takeover through weak SFTP authentication",
      "scenario": "The SFTP server allows password-only authentication without MFA or strong password requirements. An attacker brute-forces or credential-stuffs partner accounts, gains access, and uploads malicious files that corrupt order data or inject attacks targeting internal systems, causing financial loss or data breaches attributed to the legitimate partner.",
      "component": "SFTP Server",
      "likelihood": 3,
      "impact": 4,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "quote": "Partners upload CSV files of orders to our SFTP server."
        }
      ],
      "asvs": ["V2.1.1"],
      "cwe": ["CWE-521"],
      "plainLanguage": "An attacker could guess or crack a partner's password and upload fake or malicious files, disrupting operations or stealing data.",
      "requirement": {
        "title": "Require SSH key authentication for all partners",
        "given": "partners have programmatic access to the SFTP server",
        "when": "authenticating",
        "then": "disable password authentication entirely, require SSH public key authentication, and implement account lockout after repeated authentication failures"
      }
    },
    {
      "stride": "Tampering",
      "title": "Path traversal in CSV to overwrite system files",
      "scenario": "An attacker uploads a CSV with path traversal sequences in filename or reference fields (e.g., '../../etc/passwd'). If the parser writes temporary files or logs using these values without validation, the attacker could overwrite system files, escalate privileges, or plant malicious code that executes on the next system operation.",
      "component": "Parser Job",
      "likelihood": 2,
      "impact": 5,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "quote": "A parser job imports each file into the orders database."
        }
      ],
      "asvs": ["V12.3.2"],
      "cwe": ["CWE-22"],
      "plainLanguage": "An attacker could include special paths in the CSV data that trick the parser into writing to sensitive system locations, potentially compromising the server.",
      "requirement": {
        "title": "Validate all file paths from CSV before use",
        "given": "CSV may contain file references or paths",
        "when": "creating files or processing paths",
        "then": "resolve paths to their canonical form, verify they're within the expected directory, and reject any containing '..' or starting with '/' or drive letters"
      }
    },
    {
      "stride": "DenialOfService",
      "title": "Billion laughs attack through nested CSV structures",
      "scenario": "An attacker uploads a CSV with deeply nested structures or billion-laughs-style entity expansions (if parser processes XML metadata). The parser attempts to expand these, consuming all available memory and CPU, crashing the parser service and preventing any order imports until the system is manually recovered.",
      "component": "Parser Job",
      "likelihood": 2,
      "impact": 4,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "quote": "A parser job imports each file into the orders database."
        },
        {
          "quote": "Files can be up to several gigabytes."
        }
      ],
      "asvs": ["V5.5.2"],
      "cwe": ["CWE-776"],
      "plainLanguage": "An attacker could craft a CSV that expands into enormous data structures when parsed, crashing the system through memory exhaustion.",
      "requirement": {
        "title": "Set parsing limits for CSV processing",
        "given": "untrusted partners provide CSV files",
        "when": "parsing",
        "then": "enforce maximum field size (e.g., 1MB), maximum row count (e.g., 10M), maximum nesting depth if supported, and timeout limits (e.g., 5 minutes), rejecting files that exceed these"
      }
    },
    {
      "stride": "Repudiation",
      "title": "Missing audit trail for file uploads and processing",
      "scenario": "A partner uploads incorrect or unauthorized order data, causing financial discrepancies or compliance violations. Without comprehensive audit logs, there's no proof of what was uploaded, when, or by whom. The partner denies responsibility, and the organization cannot defend against chargebacks or regulatory penalties.",
      "component": "SFTP Server",
      "likelihood": 3,
      "impact": 3,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "quote": "Partners upload CSV files of orders to our SFTP server."
        }
      ],
      "asvs": ["V7.1.1"],
      "cwe": ["CWE-778"],
      "plainLanguage": "If a partner uploads bad data, we might not be able to prove what they sent or when, making it hard to resolve disputes or investigations.",
      "requirement": {
        "title": "Log all file operations with partner attribution",
        "given": "partners upload files",
        "when": "files are uploaded, processed, or deleted",
        "then": "log partner ID, filename, size, timestamp, source IP, file hash, processing status, and any errors to a tamper-proof audit log, retained per compliance requirements"
      }
    },
    {
      "stride": "InformationDisclosure",
      "title": "Sensitive data exposure through parser error messages",
      "scenario": "When the parser encounters errors (malformed CSV, database constraint violations), it logs detailed error messages including CSV content, SQL queries, or database schema. An attacker uploads intentionally malformed files, retrieves these error logs (via shared logging dashboards or error responses), and uses them to map the database schema and plan more sophisticated attacks.",
      "component": "Parser Job",
      "likelihood": 2,
      "impact": 3,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "quote": "A parser job imports each file into the orders database."
        }
      ],
      "asvs": ["V7.4.1"],
      "cwe": ["CWE-209"],
      "plainLanguage": "Error messages from the parser might reveal database structure or other sensitive details that help an attacker plan their next move.",
      "requirement": {
        "title": "Sanitize error messages before logging",
        "given": "parsing may fail on malicious or malformed input",
        "when": "logging errors",
        "then": "log detailed debugging info only to secure internal logs, never expose SQL queries, schema details, or CSV content to partners or shared dashboards; return only generic error codes to partners"
      }
    }
  ]
}
```
